<%# Header account menu: who is signed in, and the account actions. Nothing else.

    design.md names this pattern - a native <details>/<summary> disclosure of links, not an
    ARIA menu widget with arrow-key roving. Native means it still opens without JS; the
    dropdown controller adds outside-click and Escape, and returns focus to the summary.

    Why it exists at all: nothing in the chrome said who was signed in. On a shared school
    Chromebook a student can land on a session someone else left open, and signing out was
    the last item in a sidebar that collapses behind a hamburger on a phone.

    **The trigger is the avatar and a chevron, nothing else.** The name used to sit beside it at
    lg. Initials plus the panel is what GitHub, Google, Stripe and Linear all show, and a name of
    unpredictable length in fixed chrome is what pushed this control around. The identity signal
    the menu exists for is carried by the initials at a glance and by the panel on open.

    **The panel carries name, email and role.** The email is the line that tells you *which*
    account you are in - it is why Google and Stripe both lead with it - and it matters here for
    the same reason the menu does: shared machines. Students may have no email, so that line is
    omitted rather than rendered blank.

    **No navigation lives in here.** It held "View site" in admin and "My portfolio" in the app;
    both are destinations, both are now in the sidebar where the app's Admin row already was.
    A destination hidden behind an avatar is not discoverable, and "My portfolio" was a second
    copy of a row the sidebar already had.

    No rule separates the identity block from the actions - it sits on a slate-50 tint
    instead, so the panel groups without drawing a divider. %>
<% item_class = "flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 text-sm text-slate-700 " \
                "hover:bg-slate-50 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 " \
                "focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary" %>

<%# data-testid because the sidebar's Trading floor disclosure is also a <details>/
    <summary>, so "the summary" is ambiguous in a test. %>
<details class="relative" data-controller="dropdown" data-testid="account-menu">
  <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer list-none items-center gap-2 rounded-lg py-1 pl-1 pr-2 hover:bg-black/5 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
    <%# The avatar and the chevron are both aria-hidden, so this is the control's whole
        accessible name. It names the user because the visible trigger is now initials only:
        "Account menu" alone would not say whose. %>
    <span class="sr-only">Account menu for <%= current_user.display_name %></span>
    <%= avatar_tag(current_user) %>
    <%= lucide_icon("chevron-down", class: "h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-slate-600") %>
  </summary>

  <%# w-64, not w-56: an email is longer than a name, and truncating it defeats the point of
      showing it. %>
  <div class="absolute right-0 z-20 mt-2 w-64 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white py-1 shadow-lg">
    <div class="bg-slate-50 px-4 py-3">
      <p class="truncate text-sm font-medium text-slate-900"><%= current_user.display_name %></p>
      <% if current_user.email.present? %>
        <p class="truncate text-xs text-slate-600"><%= current_user.email %></p>
      <% end %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600"><%= account_role_label(current_user) %></p>
    </div>

    <%= link_to "Sign out", destroy_user_session_path,
                data: { turbo_method: :delete },
                class: item_class %>
  </div>
</details>
